from . import T
T('1-boolean','algebra','Boolean algebra;Algebraic technique','Boolean identities and algebraic minimization',r'''
Boolean variables take only zero and one; + means OR, multiplication means AND, and a bar means NOT. These are not ordinary arithmetic operations: A+A=A and A·A=A. Identity laws include A+0=A and A·1=A; complement laws include A+Ā=1 and AĀ=0. De Morgan complements both operands while swapping AND and OR.
Minimize by applying an identity to an entire matching subexpression and preserve the original truth table. Factoring can expose complements; absorption A+AB=A removes a term already covered by another. Consensus can remove a redundant bridge term, but dropping a literal without justification can add unwanted true inputs. Complementing a sum requires complementing every term, not only one.
For GATE, write one justified transformation per line and verify on a truth assignment that stresses the changed term. Algebraic minimization seeks simpler implementation but a minimal expression need not be unique. Count literals or gates under the specified hardware cost; the fewest algebraic terms need not always mean the cheapest realization.
''','Simplify F=AB+ĀB+BC.',r'Factor common B::F=B(A+\bar A)+BC::The first two terms differ only in A.~Use complement::F=B+BC::A OR NOT A is one.~Absorb::B+BC=B::When BC is true, B is already true.', 'F=B.','For B=0 every original product is zero; for B=1 either AB or ĀB is one.')
T('1-boolean','forms','SOP;POS','Canonical SOP/POS and minterms/maxterms',r'''
A sum of products ORs together AND terms; a product of sums ANDs together OR terms. Canonical SOP uses minterms, each containing every variable and true on exactly one input row. For a minterm, a row’s zero bit uses a complemented literal and its one bit an uncomplemented literal. Canonical POS uses maxterms false on exactly one row, reversing that literal convention within an OR term.
Build SOP from true rows and POS from false rows. A noncanonical simplified expression may omit variables. The notation Σm lists true minterm indices and ΠM lists false maxterm indices under a stated variable bit order. Changing bit order changes indices even when the function’s words remain the same.
To verify an expression, test its output on both listed and unlisted rows. A frequent trap uses the minterm convention when writing a maxterm and therefore selects the opposite row. Don't equate “product of sums” with complementing an SOP without the required De Morgan transformation.
''','For A,B, output is zero only at 01. Write canonical POS.',r'Identify false row::A=0,\ B=1::Only one maxterm is required.~Construct maxterm::M_1=A+\bar B::Both literals evaluate to zero at 01.~Verify other rows::::At 00,10,11 at least one literal is one.', 'F=A+¬B=ΠM(1).','The complementary true-row SOP is Σm(0,2,3), recognizing the same function.')
T('1-kmap-tabular','maps','Karnaugh map;Dont cares','Karnaugh maps, grouping and don’t-care freedom',r'''
A Karnaugh map places minterms in Gray-code order so adjacent cells differ in one variable. Edges wrap: opposite borders are adjacent, while diagonal cells generally are not. A valid group contains a power-of-two rectangle of adjacent cells. A group of 2ᵏ minterms eliminates k varying literals, retaining variables fixed throughout it.
For SOP, cover every required one without covering a required zero. Groups may overlap and can be as large as permitted; a cell need not be covered exactly once. A don't-care marks an input whose output is unspecified under the model. It may be treated as zero or one to improve simplification, and does not have to be covered. It cannot overwrite a specified output.
Begin with essential groups and choose remaining groups to meet the stated cost. A visually large collection is invalid if it is not a permitted rectangle or power-of-two size. Always translate the selected group back to its constant-bit literals and check the represented minterms rather than relying on a diagram’s appearance alone.
''','Minimize F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,2), with don’t-cares 1,3.',r'Available grouping::::The required ones and optional cells together cover 000,001,010,011.~Identify fixed bit::A=0,\quad B,C\text{ vary}::The four-cell group eliminates B and C.~Write product::F=\bar A::This agrees with required ones and keeps required zeros false.', 'F=¬A is a valid minimized realization.','Its values on 1 and 3 are allowed to be one because those rows were unspecified, not required zero.')
T('1-kmap-tabular','tabular','Tabular method;Prime implicants','Quine–McCluskey and the prime-implicant chart',r'''
The tabular method systematically combines minterms rather than relying on a spatial map. Group binary terms by number of ones and combine terms differing in exactly one retained bit, replacing that position by a dash. Repeat combinations only when dash positions agree and the remaining patterns differ in one bit. Combined terms cover all assignments to their dashed positions.
A prime implicant cannot be further enlarged without entering a required-zero case. Mark every term used in a combination; unused terms at the end of each stage are candidates for the prime set. Don’t-cares can participate in combining but the final chart needs to cover only required ones. An essential prime covers a required minterm no other prime covers.
Select all essentials, then cover remaining columns according to the cost objective. Finding every prime does not mean every prime belongs in the final expression. A minimum cover can be nonunique. In GATE, distinguish one legal simplified cover from a proof of minimality and inspect whether cost is number of terms, literals or actual gates.
''','Combine 000,001,100,101 for variables A,B,C.',r'First combinations::000,001\to00-,\quad100,101\to10-::C varies in each pair.~Second combination::00-,10-\to-0-::Dash positions agree and only A differs.~Interpret::F=\bar B::Only B remains fixed at zero.', 'One implicant −0−, corresponding to ¬B.','It covers exactly all four inputs with B=0, with no required-zero input included.')
T('1-combinational','adders','Combinational circuits;Adders','Combinational design and binary adders',r'''
A combinational circuit’s current outputs depend only on current inputs. Design starts by specifying a truth table, deriving expressions and realizing them with gates. A half adder adds two bits: XOR gives sum and AND gives carry. A full adder adds two operand bits and a carry-in; sum is their XOR and carry is one when at least two are one.
Multi-bit ripple addition chains each carry-out into the next more significant carry-in. Its long carry propagation limits delay; carry-lookahead computes carries using generate/propagate structure. A truth-table-correct circuit can still have propagation delay and transient hazards, so Boolean function and timing behavior are separate design aspects.
For signed addition, overflow occurs when equal-sign operands produce an opposite-sign result, or equivalently when carry into the sign position differs from carry out. Carry-out alone reports unsigned behavior and is not the signed-overflow test. Always state bit width and whether operands are unsigned or two’s-complement before interpreting the result bits.
''','Add 7 and 1 in 4-bit two’s complement.',r'Encode::7=0111,\quad1=0001::Both operands are positive.~Add::0111+0001=1000::Ripple carries propagate through the lower three bits.~Interpret signed result::1000=-8::Two positives produced a negative code, proving signed overflow.', 'The bit result is 1000, with signed overflow.','The mathematical answer 8 exceeds the signed range −8…7, although it fits four-bit unsigned representation.')
T('1-combinational','selection','Multiplexers;Decoders','Multiplexers select; decoders identify input codes',r'''
A multiplexer selects one data input using binary select bits. A 2-to-1 mux outputs I₀ when S=0 and I₁ when S=1, so Y=¬S·I₀+S·I₁. A 2ᵏ-to-1 mux uses k select bits; data inputs can be constants or expressions in remaining variables to implement a function by Shannon expansion.
A decoder activates an output corresponding to its input code. An n-to-2ⁿ decoder provides one minterm per output under an active-high enabled convention. ORing selected minterm outputs implements SOP. Active-low outputs or enables change the connecting logic, so inspect the specification before assuming output ones.
To realize a function by a mux, choose select variables, evaluate the desired function under each select assignment, then use each residual function as a data input. Do not confuse decoder outputs with mux select lines. These components are combinational unless a register is explicitly added; neither stores a previous input merely because it has several terminals.
''','Implement A XOR B with a 2:1 mux selected by A.',r'Select zero branch::A=0\Rightarrow Y=B::Set I₀=B.~Select one branch::A=1\Rightarrow Y=\bar B::Set I₁=¬B.~Combine::Y=\bar AB+A\bar B::This is the XOR expression.', 'Select A; data inputs B and ¬B.','Checking both select cases proves all four input combinations without guessing a wiring pattern.')
T('1-sequential','state','Sequential circuits;Moore;Mealy','State machines, Moore and Mealy outputs',r'''
A sequential circuit has stored state, so outputs can depend on previous inputs through that state. A transition function computes next state from present state and inputs. A Moore machine’s output depends only on present state; a Mealy machine’s output depends on state and current input. Labels often place Moore outputs on states and Mealy outputs on transitions.
Design by defining what each state remembers, drawing transitions, assigning binary encodings, then deriving next-state and output logic. Equivalent states may be merged only if every future input sequence yields the same observable behavior. The number of state bits is ceiling(log₂ number of encoded states), but the bit count alone does not define transitions.
Output timing differs: a Mealy output can respond within a cycle to input changes, while a Moore output usually changes when the registered state changes. Exact latency depends on the sampling convention. In sequence detectors, decide whether overlapping occurrences are permitted and ensure fallback states retain the relevant suffix after a match.
''','Design a Mealy detector for consecutive bits 01 with overlap.',r'State meanings::::Use Z when the last bit was zero and N when no relevant zero suffix exists.~Transitions::::From N, 0→Z and 1→N. From Z, 0→Z and 1→N with output 1.~Trace 00101::::The 1s at positions 3 and 5 complete matches; all other transitions output zero.', 'Two states suffice; matches end at positions 3 and 5.','Keeping Z after another zero preserves the suffix needed for the next 1.')
T('1-sequential','storage','Flip flops;Counters','Flip-flop rules and counter construction',r'''
A flip-flop stores one bit sampled on its active clock edge. D copies D into the next Q; T toggles when T=1 and holds when T=0. JK holds at (0,0), sets at (1,0), resets at (0,1), and toggles at (1,1), with pairs ordered J,K. SR’s forbidden combination depends on its implementation and active levels.
Counters implement a repeated state sequence. A modulo-n binary counter needs at least ceiling(log₂n) bits; unused encodings require defined transitions if self-starting behavior matters. Synchronous counters update all bits on a common edge; ripple counters cascade clocks and can exhibit transient intermediate codes.
Timing constraints include clock-to-Q, combinational delay and setup before the next edge; hold checks concern minimum delay and clock relationships. Counting clock edges differs from continuously evaluating next-state combinational outputs. When tracing, compute every new state from the same old state rather than updating one bit early and using it to calculate another.
''','A T flip-flop starts at Q=1 and receives T=1,1,0.',r'First edge::Q^+=Q\oplus T=1\oplus1=0::Toggle.~Second edge::Q^+=0\oplus1=1::Toggle again.~Third edge::Q^+=1\oplus0=1::Zero T retains state.', 'Final Q=1.','Two toggles cancel; the final hold does not change the recovered original state.')
T('1-representation','fixed','Number representation;Fixed point;Arithmetic','Fixed-width integers and fixed-point scaling',r'''
Unsigned n-bit representation uses positive powers of two and ranges from zero to 2ⁿ−1. Two’s-complement assigns the most significant bit weight −2ⁿ⁻¹ and ranges from −2ⁿ⁻¹ to 2ⁿ⁻¹−1. Negation within a width inverts bits and adds one. Sign extension repeats the sign bit to preserve value when increasing width; zero extension preserves unsigned values instead.
Fixed point interprets an integer code I with f fractional bits as I/2ᶠ. Resolution is 2⁻ᶠ, and range depends on total width and signedness. Adding equal-scale codes preserves scale; multiplication creates the sum of fractional-bit counts and may need rescaling and rounding. Overflow and truncation are different errors.
Specify width at every operation and retain only permitted result bits. Decoding a signed code as unsigned changes the number, not the bits. A representable decimal fraction may not have a finite binary expansion, so exact real arithmetic and finite fixed-point storage need separate treatment. GATE questions frequently test the changed scaling after multiplication rather than just decimal-to-binary conversion.
''','An 8-bit signed fixed-point format has four fractional bits. Interpret code 11101000.',r'Decode signed integer::I=232-256=-24::The leading one indicates a negative two’s-complement integer.~Apply scale::x=-24/16=-1.5::Four fractional bits imply division by 2⁴.~Resolution::\Delta=1/16::Adjacent codes differ by 0.0625 in represented value.', 'Value −1.5; resolution 1/16.','Encoding +1.5 gives integer code 24; taking its eight-bit two’s complement gives 232, the observed bit pattern.')
T('1-representation','floating','Floating point','Floating-point fields, normalization and special values',r'''
Floating point stores sign, exponent and significand to represent a wide dynamic range. A normalized binary value has form (−1)ˢ(1.f)₂2ᵉ. IEEE single precision stores 1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits and 23 fraction bits, using exponent bias 127 for normal values and an implicit leading one. Effective significand precision is therefore 24 bits for normals.
Stored exponent zero represents zero or subnormal values, which use a different leading-bit/exponent rule. All-one exponent represents infinity for zero fraction or NaN for nonzero fraction. Do not apply the normal-value formula to those reserved fields. Positive and negative zero have distinct sign codes but compare equal under ordinary equality semantics.
Addition aligns exponents before combining significands, then normalizes and rounds. Large exponent gaps can erase a small addend, so floating addition need not be associative. Overflow, underflow and rounding are distinct. In GATE, decode exponent bias and hidden bit explicitly, and identify whether the problem uses IEEE fields or a custom format with different widths or conventions.
''','Decode a normal single-precision value with sign 0, exponent 128 and fraction .100…0.',r'Unbias exponent::e=128-127=1::The stored exponent is not the actual power.~Decode significand::(1.1)_2=1.5::Include the implicit leading one.~Combine::x=(+1)(1.5)2^1=3::The sign is positive.', 'Value 3.','If the hidden bit were forgotten, the incorrectly decoded significand 0.5 would give 1 instead.')
T('2-instructions','isa','Instruction set;Addressing modes','Instruction semantics and effective addresses',r'''
An instruction set specifies visible operations, operand forms and architectural state. An opcode selects an operation; operands may be registers, immediate constants or memory references. Instruction encoding width and field sizes determine how many operation/operand choices can be named. A program’s meaning follows the instruction semantics, not the appearance of an assembly mnemonic alone.
An addressing mode tells how to obtain the operand or its effective address. Immediate uses a value in the instruction; register uses a register value; direct uses a stated memory address; indirect follows an address through a register or memory. Base-plus-displacement adds an offset, indexed/scaled modes add an element index times element width, and PC-relative uses a defined PC reference.
Separate effective-address computation from fetching memory contents at that address. A question saying register R contains 1000 does not say memory[1000] contains 1000. PC-relative reference may use the next instruction address under the ISA, so check the stated convention. For encodings, account for unused field patterns and variable instruction formats before multiplying apparent possibilities.
''','R=1000, displacement=12, memory[1012]=42. What does a base-displacement load obtain?',r'Address::EA=R+12=1012::Base and displacement locate the memory cell.~Memory access::operand=memory[1012]=42::The load reads its contents, not the address itself.~Contrast immediate::::An immediate 12 would supply the constant 12 without this memory lookup.', 'The loaded operand is 42.','1012 is the effective address, not the fetched value; confusing them changes the instruction’s meaning.')
T('2-instructions','performance','CPU performance','Instruction count, CPI and clock-rate comparisons',r'''
CPU execution time is instruction count times average cycles per instruction divided by cycles per second. Instruction count depends on the program and ISA/compiler choices; CPI depends on instruction mix and implementation; clock rate converts cycles to time. A higher frequency alone does not guarantee a faster program if CPI or instruction count also changes.
Weighted CPI uses dynamic instruction fractions: ΣpᵢCPIᵢ. Static source counts do not measure how often loops execute. Speedup is old time/new time, so a value above one means improvement. Amdahl’s law limits gains when only a fraction of execution is accelerated; time outside that fraction remains.
Convert GHz to 10⁹ cycles/s and keep instructions, cycles and seconds separate. Do not average performance rates where elapsed times should be added. For comparing architectures, calculate complete program time under each instruction count and CPI before taking their ratio. A “20% faster” rate and a “20% less time” statement are not identical percentages.
''','A executes 1 million instructions at CPI 2 and 1 GHz; B at CPI 3 and 2 GHz with the same count.',r'Time A::T_A=10^6(2)/10^9=2\ ms::Count cycles then divide by rate.~Time B::T_B=10^6(3)/(2\cdot10^9)=1.5\ ms::Its higher CPI partly offsets its clock gain.~Speedup::T_A/T_B=4/3::Compare elapsed times.', 'B is faster by a speedup of 4/3.','B’s twofold clock rate does not yield twofold program speed because each instruction uses more cycles.')
T('2-alu-control','alu','Design of ALU','ALU design: arithmetic, logic and flags',r'''
An ALU combines operand paths for arithmetic and bitwise logic, selecting one output using control signals. An n-bit adder can support subtraction by conditionally inverting the second operand and inserting carry-in one, because A−B=A+¬B+1 modulo 2ⁿ. Separate AND/OR/XOR networks implement bitwise operations; a result multiplexer chooses the requested function.
Carry chains determine addition delay; faster structures use propagate/generate relationships. Flags describe different aspects of the result: zero checks all result bits, sign reads the leading bit, carry concerns unsigned arithmetic and overflow concerns signed representability. Shifts need stated logical or arithmetic fill behavior; arithmetic right shift normally repeats the sign bit.
Control decoding must drive both operand transformation and output selection consistently. A circuit giving correct low bits can still set a wrong overflow flag. In a trace, use the exact width and operation convention. Two’s-complement subtraction’s final carry interpretation depends on whether the architecture treats carry as no-borrow or exposes a separate borrow flag.
''','Subtract 3 from 5 using a four-bit adder.',r'Prepare operands::A=0101,\quad\bar B=1100::Invert 0011 at the same width.~Add with carry-in::0101+1100+1=10010::This implements subtraction modulo 16.~Retain result::low_4=0010=2::Discard the fifth result bit but analyze flags separately.', 'Result 2, with no signed overflow.','The true signed result lies in −8…7; carry-out is not a reason to label it signed overflow.')
T('2-alu-control','control','Hardwired control;Microprogrammed control','Hardwired versus microprogrammed control units',r'''
The control unit schedules datapath actions such as register loads, ALU selections and memory reads. Hardwired control implements a state machine using combinational decoding and state registers. It can be fast but changing the instruction sequencing often requires changing hardware logic. Microprogrammed control stores sequences of microinstructions in a control memory and uses a microsequencer to choose their order.
A microinstruction can assert several independent control signals during one microcycle, provided the datapath supports them without conflict. Horizontal formats use wider explicit controls; vertical formats encode groups more compactly and need decoding. Control-store capacity is number of stored words times word width, not the architectural program-memory capacity.
An ISA instruction may execute several microinstructions, and their count need not equal the number of assembly instructions. A microprogram does not remove the need for datapath hardware or timing constraints. Compare the chosen design’s speed, encoding and modifiability rather than claiming every microprogrammed machine is necessarily slow. Trace which register updates occur simultaneously and which require a later microstep.
''','A control memory has 256 microinstructions, each 48 bits. Find storage bytes.',r'Count bits::256\cdot48=12288\ bits::Every stored word uses 48 bits.~Convert::12288/8=1536\ bytes::Eight bits form one byte.~Identify scope::::This excludes sequencer and decoder circuitry and any additional metadata.', '1536 bytes, or 1.5 KiB.','The address field needs eight bits to select 256 words; that is not the width of each microinstruction.')
T('2-cache','hierarchy','Memory hierarchy;Cache performance','Memory hierarchy and average access time',r'''
A hierarchy trades small fast storage against larger slower storage. Temporal locality predicts reuse of recently accessed data; spatial locality predicts access to nearby locations. A cache transfers blocks rather than individual requested bytes so it can exploit both. A hit finds the block in cache; a miss fetches it from a lower level and may evict another block.
Average access time depends on how miss cost is defined. With hit time Tₕ and additional penalty P, AMAT=Tₕ+miss_rate·P, since every access pays the baseline and misses add penalty. If instead supplied hit and miss times are complete mutually exclusive path times, use hit_rate·T_hit+miss_rate·T_miss. Mixing these conventions double-counts or omits time.
Multiple levels require conditional miss rates: an L2 local miss rate is among requests reaching L2, not among all CPU references. Dirty write-back blocks can add eviction work; write-through and write-allocate choices alter traffic. State what capacity and timing include before calculating. A high hit rate can still have poor AMAT when rare misses are exceptionally costly.
''','Hit time 1 ns, miss rate 5%, extra miss penalty 60 ns.',r'Baseline::::Every access first spends 1 ns in the cache lookup.~Expected extra cost::0.05(60)=3\ ns::Only the miss fraction pays the additional penalty.~Total::AMAT=1+3=4\ ns::Add baseline and expected increment.', 'AMAT=4 ns.','Complete miss path is 61 ns; 0.95×1+0.05×61 also yields 4 ns.')
T('2-cache','mapping','Cache mapping','Direct, associative and set-associative mapping',r'''
A byte-addressed cache divides an address into block offset, set index and tag. Offset chooses a byte within a block; index chooses a set; tag distinguishes different memory blocks using that set. For data capacity C, block bytes B and W ways, there are C/B lines and C/(BW) sets. With power-of-two sizes the corresponding bit counts are logarithms.
Direct mapping has one way per set, fully associative mapping one set, and set associativity several candidate lines per indexed set. Replacement selects among ways of the chosen set, not arbitrary lines from the whole cache. LRU records recent references within that set. Valid bits prevent an uninitialized tag from being mistaken for resident data.
For a reference trace, first divide byte address by block size to identify block number, then take modulo set count. Nearby bytes in one block can hit after one miss. Cache capacity normally means data capacity unless metadata is explicitly included. Distinguish compulsory, conflict and capacity misses; increasing associativity targets conflicts without necessarily changing the block offset.
''','A direct-mapped 16-byte cache uses 4-byte blocks. Access byte addresses 0,3,16.',r'Sets::S=16/4=4::There is one line per set.~First two accesses::::Addresses 0 and 3 are block 0, set 0: the first misses, the second hits.~Third access::block(16)=4,\quad4\bmod4=0::A different tag maps to set 0 and replaces block 0.', 'Miss, hit, miss; the last access is a mapping conflict.','Addresses 0 and 3 differ only in block offset, while 16 identifies a different block with the same set index.')
T('2-memory-io','chips','Memory interfacing','Memory chips, word width and address decoding',r'''
A chip described as D×W stores D addressable words, each W bits. To increase width, connect chips in parallel to common address signals and use different data-bit lanes. To increase depth, create banks and use higher address bits to enable the selected bank. These operations are independent, so compatible expansion factors multiply.
Address bits select word locations, not individual stored bits unless the chip is bit-addressed. Within a power-of-two depth D, log₂D bits address each location. Bank-select bits are additional to within-chip bits. Read/write and chip-enable signals must prevent several banks from driving the same data bus simultaneously.
Check unit conventions: a 1K×8 chip has 1024 byte-wide words, whereas 1K×1 has 1024 single-bit words. A target capacity expressed in bytes needs conversion to its access width before choosing factors. Unused address ranges and partial decoding can create aliases; the desired memory map may require full decoding rather than just enough pins to store the requested bit count.
''','Construct 8K×16 from 2K×8 chips.',r'Width expansion::16/8=2::Two chips provide each 16-bit word.~Depth expansion::8K/2K=4::Four banks provide the locations.~Total and addressing::N=2\cdot4=8,\quad a_{chip}=11,\ a_{bank}=2::Select within a 2048-word chip and among four banks.', 'Eight chips; thirteen word-address bits overall.','8×2048×8 bits equals 8192×16 bits, confirming total capacity.')
T('2-memory-io','io','I/O interface;Interrupt;DMA','Programmed I/O, interrupts and DMA',r'''
An I/O interface exposes device data, status and control registers, reached through memory-mapped or separate I/O addressing. Programmed polling repeatedly reads status until the device is ready, then the CPU transfers data. Interrupt-driven I/O lets the device request service, so the CPU can do other work rather than poll continuously; the interrupt handler still performs work and has entry/exit overhead.
DMA allows a controller to transfer a block between device and memory after CPU setup. It needs bus arbitration and can occupy memory bandwidth; the CPU is not necessarily idle or entirely unaffected during transfer. Completion can raise an interrupt. DMA reduces per-unit CPU transfer involvement, not the physical number of bytes moved.
Trace who copies data, who controls the address/count and when the CPU is notified. An interrupt is a control event, not automatically a DMA transfer. Bus stealing can delay CPU memory accesses even while saving software overhead. In performance questions include setup, transfer and completion costs exactly as specified instead of assuming one mode has zero overhead.
''','Transfer 4096 bytes with one interrupt per byte versus one DMA block completion.',r'Byte service count::N_{interrupt}=4096::Each byte separately asks the CPU for service.~DMA setup::::The CPU supplies starting address, length and direction to the controller.~DMA completion::::One block completion notification replaces thousands of byte handlers under this model.', 'DMA greatly reduces CPU service events; all 4096 bytes still cross the transfer path.','Saving handler work does not mean the device-to-memory transfer itself took zero time.')
T('2-pipeline','overlap','Instruction pipelining','Pipeline fill, steady throughput and drain',r'''
A pipeline partitions instruction processing into stages and overlaps different instructions at those stages. It improves throughput without necessarily reducing an individual instruction’s latency. With k balanced stages and n instructions in an initially empty ideal single-issue pipeline, completion takes k+n−1 cycles: the first instruction uses k and each later one adds one.
The clock period must accommodate the slowest stage plus pipeline-register overhead. Dividing unpipelined work into k stages does not guarantee k-fold speedup when delays are unbalanced, n is small or overhead matters. Fill/drain effects are significant for short sequences, while long ideal sequences approach one instruction per cycle.
Stalls insert bubbles and increase elapsed cycles. Flushes discard incorrectly fetched work and can add costs. Count actual extra cycles once under the stated model; do not multiply a total stall count by every instruction. GATE questions may ask cycles, nanoseconds, throughput or speedup, so convert only after deciding which quantity the model gives.
''','Four stages each clocked at 3 ns run ten instructions without stalls.',r'Ideal cycles::C=4+10-1=13::Include first-instruction fill.~Elapsed time::T=13(3)=39\ ns::Every cycle has the stated period.~Single instruction latency::L=4(3)=12\ ns::This differs from the steady completion interval of 3 ns.', 'Total 39 ns; individual ideal latency 12 ns.','For n=1 the same formula gives four cycles, establishing that pipeline fill was included.')
T('2-pipeline','hazards','Pipeline hazards;Forwarding;Branches','Hazards, forwarding and branch recovery',r'''
A structural hazard arises when overlapping instructions require the same unavailable resource. A data hazard arises when an instruction needs a value before the producing instruction can supply it. A control hazard arises when the next instruction address depends on an unresolved branch. These categories describe causes; bubbles, duplication and prediction are remedies with specific limits.
Forwarding bypasses a value from a producing stage to a consuming stage without waiting for register-file writeback. It cannot send a value before it exists, so a load-use dependency may still need a stall when memory data arrives too late. In a basic in-order pipeline, common operand dependencies are read-after-write; other hazard types depend on ordering and execution structure.
For branches, a misprediction flushes wrong-path work. Expected added CPI can be branch fraction times conditional misprediction fraction times penalty cycles, provided costs are modeled without overlap. Trace stage timing before asserting that forwarding eliminates every dependency stall, and distinguish branch frequency from misprediction frequency among branches.
''','Base CPI 1; branches 25%, prediction wrong on 20% of branches, penalty 4 cycles.',r'Mispredictions per instruction::0.25(0.20)=0.05::The two probabilities refer to different populations.~Added cycles per instruction::\Delta CPI=0.05(4)=0.20::Multiply conditional event frequency by penalty.~Effective CPI::CPI=1+0.20=1.20::No other stalls are assumed.', 'Effective CPI 1.2.','Applying the 20% rate to all instructions would overcount because only one quarter are branches.')
